#{[17592186162972 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162972 #edb/ref 1061 33961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe3d0b73-cb7e-5370-9455-c490da334890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162973 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162973 #edb/ref 1061 33963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162973 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09f12104-7949-5867-9884-48e422a32857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162973 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162974 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162974 #edb/ref 1061 33964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162974 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65bbda75-37ec-5809-926a-0fb8d31c008d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162974 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162975 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162975 #edb/ref 1061 33966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55f9fd86-1f4a-5f2c-b7c2-a60113a4a9e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162976 #edb/ref 1061 33968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e17beec2-91ed-5375-9c58-ab279784f2bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162976 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162977 #edb/ref 1061 33969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162977 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da23e59c-3020-5db2-8f5b-77675938bf0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162977 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163273 #edb/ref 1064 "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163273 #edb/ref 1065 5458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163273 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6eb17ec7-1e57-5d75-8a71-9030a420602b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163273 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163274 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163274 #edb/ref 1065 5459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163274 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d33b6982-066e-5c82-8138-0283ad8dccbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163274 #edb/ref 1067 "The Variance of a Scaled Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163275 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163275 #edb/ref 1065 5460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163275 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1523e51b-84da-5100-809f-09fbcb0d80ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163275 #edb/ref 1067 "The Variance of a Transformed Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163276 #edb/ref 1064 "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163276 #edb/ref 1065 5461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163276 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0372f0de-b6a6-58ee-8195-c04024e8f745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163276 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the General Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "202fca4b-d4db-559c-86f9-155db3d13c64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238442 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238442 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238442 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b56c1db-87eb-5ec9-914d-30a522b26fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238911 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6182bb36-df22-5b55-8de2-18be6f4e6edb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239517 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a846d2c7-f2b2-52b6-aa5d-db3effda179c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240218 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240218 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240218 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f503a4ab-d69a-5e93-a2e0-b67020545fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186238911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240360 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "13f36fa9-d65b-5d54-8f7f-9bbf30419197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240618 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a3174f2e-e4fe-5b15-9ca8-8ba15281d2ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A=\\{1,2,4,8,16\\}$ and $B=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16\\},$ then $B∖A=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1c945ab-a73d-5e4c-9433-39a80d9da5a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241013 #edb/ref 1074 "The set $B∖A$ is the set of all elements belonging to $B$ but *not* $A.$ Note that the elements $2,4,8,16\\in A$ are also in $B,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∖A & =B-A \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16\\}-\\{1,2,4,8,16\\} \\\\  & =\\{6,10,12,14\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,-2,-1⟩$, $c=⟨-4,5,-1⟩$, and  $u=⟨1,1,4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caedc8c2-ba3c-5e61-ad1f-0d5eafbb8652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241023 #edb/ref 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-4t=1 \\\\ -2s+5t=1 \\\\ -s-t=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-4t=1 \\\\ t=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-4t & =1 \\\\ s-4(-1) & =1 \\\\ s+4 & =1 \\\\ s & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-3,\\,t=-1,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=-3b-c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-3)\\cdot (-1)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242081 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242081 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242081 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "643b59ab-e862-5975-86dd-7355eed4cca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242163 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [